            <ArticleTitle>Acute coronary syndrome following repair of aortic dissection.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>860-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Cardiac complications associated with type A aortic dissections are relatively common before and during the surgical repair. A cardiac event occurring a few days after routine recovery is rare though. We describe a case of acute occlusion of the left anterior descending artery by thrombus, 4 days after surgical repair, salvaged by emergency angioplasty and stenting. A possible explanation for the thrombus includes embolisation of a fragment of glue.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
